{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787080651,"nonce":"9xBIgUkbLikpOVXoQ5F0/H1deYI=","title":"The digest ships with no layout: its headline is smuggled through `Group.Title` so a renderer that has never heard of a digest draws something true, and that is scaffolding rather than a design","message":"**What is wrong.** `ViewService.digest` (`internal/notification/service/view.go:143-152`) builds a `NotificationView` whose entire content is `Group: GroupView{Title: DigestHeadline(n, \"\", 0), State: \"open\"}`. There is no digest arm anywhere in `internal/channels/render/slack` — the word does not appear in the package outside tests — so the card is drawn by the `default:` branch as `*Title* — \u003cstatus\u003e`, and a digest reaches Slack as a generic info card whose only content is one sentence in the slot reserved for a group's name.\n\n**The demonstration.** The code says it, without being asked. `view.go:118-125`: *\"⚠️ THE HEADLINE GOES WHERE THE GROUP TITLE GOES, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE PIECE OF SCAFFOLDING … Putting the sentence there makes a digest render as a plain, correct info card on a renderer that has never heard of one.\"* And `:139-142`: *\"A renderer that learns to lay a digest out should read `DigestCount` and `DigestWindowStart` off the notification. **This is a floor, not a design**.\"*\n\nThe choice was forced rather than preferred, and the reason is worth reading: an empty title would produce an empty `rendered_fallback` and fail `deliveries_fb_ck` with a 23514 *\"after the message had already gone to Slack, which is the worst place in the module to fail\"* (`:120-124`). So `Group.Title` is not where a digest headline belongs; it is the one field that could not be left empty.\n\nWhat the floor costs is enumerated at `:127-138` and each item is a real absence, not a stylistic one. **No actions** — a digest is about a window, and a button would have to pick one of the things it counted. **No links** — every deep link is `/alerts/\u003cid\u003e` or `/groups/\u003cid\u003e` and a digest names neither, so *\"a link to a filtered list is the right answer and it needs a filter vocabulary this view does not have.\"* **No org** — the footer loses the org name. The result is a card that reports a number and offers no way to see what the number is about. `DigestCount` and `DigestWindowStart` are on the notification row (migration 00058), unread by any renderer.\n\n**Why it matters.** The digest is the reason `00058` exists: one summary per window per namespace, replacing per-event traffic. What an operator receives is a plain grey line of prose in a thread — no count laid out, no window boundaries as fields, no severity breakdown, no route to the cases counted. Compared with the root card the same channel gets for a single alert, the digest is the least designed thing oto sends, and it is the one that stands in for the most.\n\nIt also carries the shape of a future defect. `Group.Title` on a digest view is a group field holding something that is not a group's title, and `Group.State: \"open\"` is a group state on a view with no group. Anything that later reads either field generically — a fallback derivation, an audit surface, a second renderer — will read a digest's headline as a group name and be right by accident until it is not.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/service/view.go:114-142` (the doc comment: the scaffolding, the three deliberate absences, and \"a floor, not a design\"), `:143-152` (`digest`) · `DigestHeadline` (same package — the one sentence a digest currently is) · `internal/channels/render/slack/reply.go` and `root.go` (the `default:` arms that draw it; there is no digest branch to cite, which is the point) · `internal/notification/domain/notification.go:228`, `:247` (`Digest()`, `DigestCount`, `DigestWindowStart` — the facts a layout would read) · `db/migrations/00058_a_digest_is_a_window_over_a_namespace.sql` (where those columns and the `(channel_id, 'digest', policy_id)` thread come from) · `internal/notification/service/dispatch.go:739` (the digest arm that already exists in the dispatcher, so the renderer is the layer that is missing one).\n\n**Scope.** medium. The renderer work is ordinary; what precedes it is not. A digest layout needs the count and window laid out as fields rather than prose, a decision on what a digest links to — which means either a filter vocabulary the view does not have, or accepting that it links to nothing — and a decision on whether the headline keeps riding `Group.Title` or the view grows a field of its own, which is a change to `NotificationView`'s shape and therefore to every renderer.\n\n**Done when.** A digest is laid out as a digest: its count and its window are rendered as facts rather than embedded in one sentence, and the card is recognisable as a summary rather than as a group card with an unusual title. Whether it links anywhere is decided rather than deferred, and if it does not, the card says what to look at instead. The headline no longer occupies `Group.Title` on a view that has no group — or, if it still does, one comment owns that decision and every generic reader of `Group.Title` is checked against it. A golden covers the digest card, so the next change to it is a diff rather than a discovery. `view.go:139-141`'s \"this is a floor, not a design\" is deleted, because there is a design.","files":null}]}